// PALLET TOWER MIDSUMMER (working name; the title shown is the owner's). A WORLD MADE AFTER A PLACE AND ITS NIGHT, not that place
// rebuilt whole: Slinningsbålet, the midsummer bonfire of Ålesund, a round tower of pallets 47.4 m tall as in the record year,
// 2016, on a rock islet off the point, lit from the top in the low sun of the lighting hour. We fly once round it while the fire
// comes down the stack and the evening goes to the blue dusk of the white night, and leave past where it has fallen into the sea.
// NO COUNT OF THE REAL TOWER'S PALLETS IS SAID ANYWHERE; what is drawn is counted. NOBODY IS ON THE STACK at any stage; the
// climber who lights the barrel is not shown. NOTHING IS LETTERED: no flag, name, number or mark on stack, boats or shore.
//
// WHAT STANDS (the design's stages A to E and its mend rounds F to L and O, each rule written before its picture; its rounds M,
// N, P and P14 mended words, no law; its later rounds C and P13 mended law AFTER pictures: faults seen in them, the readers'
// findings and the owner's terms on the eye): the flight and the gaze, the hour's law whole, the sea, the ground, the stack
// and THE BURN (the
// fire's states on the stack, flames, smoke, sparks, the cascade, the pallets that are shed, the fire's light), THE ROAD OF FIRE
// and the stack's image on the sea, THE FLEET, THE PEOPLE AND THE LAMPS, THE FALL (the stack breaks into five lengths and falls
// into the sea toward bearing 160: its sparks, smoke, steam and spray, the pallets that leave it, the wave and the foam), and THE
// SHORE (the boathouses and white houses of the point with their doors and windows, the town across the water, the long ship, the
// trees, the gulls). Nothing in the world is a stand-in. PREVIEWS WERE USED (section 172 b), of an earlier state; two pre-readers
// and their two checkers read that state too; what they found is mended or said here. In the round after the preview the break's
// lean (8 degrees) and the gaze's hold (2 s) and ease (6.5 s) after the strike were typed anew (the design's rules F1 and F6,
// worded by seconds and things).
//
// FRAME: x east, z = minus north, y up from the sea (level 0). A BEARING is degrees clockwise from north, seen from the stack's
//   axis (the origin); the kit sky's azimuth is the same bearing. A STATION is the metre of the route where the eye crosses a
//   compass bearing from the axis (found on the route by the sum below, never typed as a metre; the east's bearing alone is also
//   a typed knot of the route). THE JOURNEY'S SECOND T at a metre is the flight table's own time there: a pure function of the
//   metre flown, 0 at the entry and 60 at the ring.
//
// SECTION 153 (the owner's term 1 and its term 6 as it now reads): THE HOUR goes by the metre flown through T (ONE smooth law
//   for the sun, the sky, the sea's light, the fog, the far mountains and the world's own light; it does not run while the way
//   stands still), AND SO DOES THE BURN: how far the front has come down, each course's age, what chars, what is shed and where
//   it flies, the crown flame's height, how much smoke and how many sparks there are, the fire's light, the top's eating and
//   the barrel's burning away (both by the front's depth under the top). AND SO DOES THE FALL: the release, the rock back, the
//   rod's lean, the break and the five lengths' leans, each length's touch and landing and its crush, the bursts of sparks, the
//   beginning of the steam and of the spray, the pallets that leave and are thrown, the ring wave and the foam's reach and
//   going (the foam's churn is the road's small ripples, which go by the wrapped clock), the fire's light going over to the
//   wreck: all by T alone, the same for every visitor; no seed deals the lengths' law. A SPARK'S OR A PUFF'S BIRTH SECOND is T
//   less its own age by the wrapped clock: one born on the falling stack starts on its own length as that length then was. A
//   puff's place changes at once as its birth second passes the strike (from its own length to the fallen line; a visitor's
//   pace need not be 1), and there the puff is not shown: its strength goes smoothly to nought as its birth second nears the
//   strike and is whole again 0.4 s either side of it. A FRAGMENT OF THE CASCADE starts at a share of the depth the front had
//   reached 4 s before the second it is drawn (the depth down to which courses shed), so its start slides down a little during
//   its flight: a pure law of T; the cascade goes out from 1 s before the release to 0.5 s after it, by T.
//   WHAT IDLES GOES BY THE WORLD'S CLOCK: UNWRAPPED, as the engine hands it (the kit's own laws): the kit's sea
//   and its sky's cloud drift (the kit's ground is handed the same clock); and by the kit sea's own waves and clock the waves
//   under the lamps' streaks, the road of fire and the stack's image, and the boats' rocking (bounded: heave 0.20 m and lean
//   8.31 degrees at the very most for the smallest boat, by the sum); and by the clock wrapped at 600 s, every rate a whole
//   number of turns in it: the flames' flicker, the sparks' rise, the smoke's climb, the small ripples of the fire's road, the
//   dashes and the waver of the lamps' streaks, the flicker of the fire's light, the steam's hiss (each puff born again and
//   again, shown from up to 0.3 s after its length's touch), the bob of the wreck's pallets, the drift of the smoke's and the
//   steam's second noise and the turning of the smoke's puffs, the ember's drift and pulse in the core and the heart, the
//   fire's flicker in the shell's openings, the ragged height of the flames on the fallen lengths, THE ONE BOAT UNDER WAY (a
//   closed course over open water, once round in the wrap at 3.68 m/s: an arc 262 m from the axis from bearing 285 round by the
//   south to bearing 80, a half turn of 25 m radius, back on an arc 312 m from the axis, a half turn; its wake is a straight
//   band behind it, through its half turns too) and THE GULLS (140, each for ever on a closed circle of its own: its
//   turns, its wing beats and its glides whole numbers in the wrap, its bob a whole number of waves in a turn).
//   THE LAMPS (section 84) come on BY THE SUN'S HEIGHT, which is the hour's: each boat, each house and the long ship at a
//   threshold of its own between 0.7 degrees over the horizon and 3.3 under, dealt from the lamps' own stream, eased over the
//   degree about it (a boat's lamps and its cabin's windows share one, as do a white house's two lamps and the long ship's forty;
//   a town house's street lamp has its own). A WHITE HOUSE'S WINDOWS come on the same way: six in ten of them, each at a
//   threshold of its own in the same range, by a hash of its house's place and its own number, eased over the degree about it. No
//   lamp and no window comes on at a typed share of the way; none is lit before the south-south-east station.
//   EVERY READ OF THE EYE'S PLACE, NAMED (section 153):
//   FILTERS BY A PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT, each a slope taken across neighbouring pixels: the shell's courses go to their mean as a
//   course shrinks from 3.3 to 1.4 pixels in height, and as a pallet shrinks from 6.7 to 2.9 pixels along its course; the road of
//   fire and the stack's image leave out a wave or ripple their pixel cannot hold and widen the image by the lost slope, each
//   keeping its sum across (the road's widening, below); THE FALL'S RING WAVE AND FOAM are NOT thinned by a pixel's footprint
//   (rule O3): they take the road's fog as the rest of the road does (the air between, below), and the foam's churn is a lookup
//   of the noise texture, filtered as every such lookup; a lamp's streak widens and dims its glint by the slope its pixel cannot
//   hold; its dashes, as every lookup of the fire's noise texture in a fragment stage, go to their mean through the texture's own
//   smaller copies (what the pits and the sleeve's holes do about the spread so lost is named below); a house's window or door is
//   drawn as the mean, over a footprint, of the slit it is along its wall and up it (a door has no lower edge): the footprint is
//   twice the summed slopes of its two measures across neighbouring pixels (some four pixels on a wall seen square on) and never
//   more than a metre, twice the least room a window keeps to its wall's end and to the roof's line. The mean's sum across the
//   wall is the slit's own width at every footprint, so a window keeps its whole area, and a lit one its whole light, at every
//   footprint (windows/paper.txt), but for the upper outer corner of a gable window under the roof's line: at most 0.0015 m2 of a
//   window's 1.2 falls off the wall there. THE PLAIN LIMIT, KEPT: where a pixel is wider than a metre of wall the footprint is
//   narrower than the pixel, and the pixel shows the mean over a metre about its own middle, not its own mean (how often the lens
//   meets it is not counted). The honest mend would be the wall's own mean under such a pixel, and is not made. NOT MENDED: the
//   soft cuts taken of lookups of the fire's noise elsewhere (among them a tongue of flame's, the eaten edge of a puff of smoke
//   or steam, a pallet's ember patches, the foam's churn and the eating of the tops of the shell, the core and the heart) are not
//   widened: where a pixel holds many texels the lookup has gone toward its mean, and the cut of a mean is not the mean of the
//   cut. In the crew's refuter's sum over the whole texture and its smaller copies (words/head-refute/refute.txt, its part on the
//   cuts) a tongue's holes and a puff's eaten edge fill in, the foam's churn grows, a pallet's patches thin, the eating of the
//   shell's top grows less and the core's keeps its share (the heart is cut as the core is); how much of this the lens meets at
//   its own distances is not counted. The honest mend would widen each such cut by the spread its lookups have lost, as the
//   sleeve's is widened (below), and is not made. Five fixed cuts of a faint cover stay: a tongue of flame's and the wake's under
//   0.004, a puff of smoke's and of steam's under 0.003, and the road's where its cover and its red are both under 0.004, taken
//   before the air. Each cut's level is fixed; the cover it is taken of has read the lens only as this head names: the road's
//   through the mirror and the filters by a pixel's footprint, a tongue's and a puff's through the filter of their lookups, the
//   wake's not at all.
//   THE ROAD'S WIDENING: the fire's amount falls as its image widens, so that its sum across is kept, and its colour is read from
//   the WIDENED shape before that fall, so that the centre line keeps its colour at every width and the summed light of each
//   colour is kept. THIS IS NOT AS THE CREW'S NOTE TO THE OWNER ON THE EYE'S TERMS HAD IT (notes-out/owner-eye-terms.txt, its
//   point 6, where the crew said what its code would do: the colour read from the unwidened shape); read so, the core colour's
//   summed light would fall as the image widens. The stack's image spreads its soft edge about the same half-way mark until the
//   edge's inner end reaches the axis, and from there on it is scaled once, by the width it had there over the width it has, so
//   that its sum across is kept. NOT MENDED: where the fire's image lies over the stack's, the stack's light seen under it,
//   summed across, RISES as the image widens, for the widened fire covers it less (the crew's refuter's sum,
//   masts-road/refute.txt, its print masts-road/refute_extra.out.txt: up to 1.66 times what it is at no widening, at a widening
//   of three of the stack's radii, where the fire's amount on its centre line is 1 at no widening; up to 1.17 times where that
//   amount is 0.4: less where the fire is fainter); the honest mend would widen the two as one,
//   the stack's image already covered by the fire, and is not made.
//   THE PITS of the stack's shell and core are a hard cut of two lookups of the fire's noise, and the same cut of a lookup
//   broader than a texel closes the pits as the footprint grows. So, on faces seen from outside, the two lookups are taken at a
//   level of the texture's smaller copies that the stage reckons itself from the pixel's footprint (from the copy 0 to the copy
//   6), and where the footprint is over a texel of either lookup the cut is a COVER: the share of the unfiltered noise that lies
//   under the threshold, reckoned as a normal share from a typed table (for each copy its tie to the unfiltered noise, its
//   spread, its tie to the next copy and its mean) and from this machine's own last copy, so that the wall keeps its pitted share
//   (its mean cover, taken over the whole texture and over the whole wall, within 0.011 of the true share at every threshold and
//   footprint the crew summed: pits/paper.txt and pits/refute.txt). A wholly pitted pixel is dropped; a part-pitted pixel of the
//   shell shows its wall mixed by the cover with the ember that stands straight inward of it (at the same turn and height, not
//   along the ray to the eye: the core, and through the core's pits the heart), the pit's glowing rim eased as the cut is; a
//   part-pitted pixel of the core shows its ember mixed by the cover with the heart's. Where a pixel's footprint is under a texel
//   of both lookups the cut is the hard cut it was. KEPT, AND BY THE LAW'S LETTER STILL A FAULT: the hard cut of a broadened
//   lookup stays on the shell's faces seen from inside, on the core's faces seen from behind, and at a part-pitted pixel of an
//   outside face where no ember (the shell's) or no heart (the core's) is known to stand behind: there the pits still close to
//   nothing as the footprint grows. What the ray meets behind those pits is not known to the stage; the honest mend would lay
//   such a pixel by its cover over it (the wall blended and drawn in its order), or cut the unfiltered noise there (the share
//   kept; a sparkle as the lens moves is to be expected of it, and is not counted), and neither is made.
//   THE SLEEVE OF FLAME'S HOLES are a steep cut of a heat made of two such lookups, and a heat so filtered has lost spread: the
//   cut is WIDENED by the spread lost under the pixel (a typed table, summed once over the noise texture and its copies), so that
//   a pixel shows the share of cover its texels would: the mean cover within 0.015 of the unfiltered one at every cover and size
//   of pixel summed (the crew's refuter's sum: sleeve/refute.txt). NOT MENDED: the sleeve's colour is the colour of the filtered
//   heat and not the mean of its texels' colours; in the same refuter's sum (the sleeve above the front, seen square on, a pixel
//   taken as 0.0011 of its distance) its light fell short of the truth: 0.91 of the true red and 0.81 of the true green at 120 m
//   from the lens, 0.59 and 0.34 at 2,000 m; the honest mend would be the mean of the colour over the spread the heat has lost,
//   and is not made.
//   FLOORS THAT ARE TYPED SHARES OF THE DISTANCE FROM THE LENS (one pixel of a picture 1,080 lines high at the typed field of 62
//   degrees is 0.0011 of the distance; not a measured footprint): a spark's size is no smaller than 0.0011 of its distance and it
//   is dimmed by as much as it is drawn larger; a fragment of the cascade is no narrower than 0.0011, dimmed by as much; a spark
//   of the fall's burst no smaller than 0.0018, dimmed by as much; a lamp is a glow of its dealt size (drawn 2.6 times that
//   across, level to a fifth of the way out and fading to its edge) and, in the glow's level middle, a core a fifth of that size
//   that holds 59 times the glow's whole light: neither is taken smaller than 0.0014 of the lamp's distance, and each is dimmed
//   by as much as its face is drawn larger, so that each keeps its whole light: before the ceiling named next, a lamp's whole
//   light is 60 times its glow's at every distance; the half width of a lamp's streak is no less than 0.0012 of the distance from
//   the lens to the streak's farthest water (its end by the lamp), DIMMED BY AS MUCH.
//   A LAMP'S CEILING, AND WHAT IT DOES: a lamp adds to a pixel no more than 2.6 of its dealt colour: a ceiling taken at each
//   pixel, after the air, and each lamp's own (lamps that lie over one another still add). Under it, the air apart, a lamp shows
//   a sixtieth of its whole light (its glow) while it is nearer than a fifth of the distance at which the floor begins to widen
//   its glow (its dealt size over 0.0014), and all of its light from 7.75 times that distance on; between that distance and 7.75
//   times it the lamp's light at the lens falls 2.35 times where the inverse square falls 60 times, and it falls at every step of
//   the way. SO THE SHARE OF A LAMP'S LIGHT THAT IS SHOWN RISES WITH ITS DISTANCE FROM THE LENS. THIS IS NOT AS THE CREW'S NOTE
//   HAD IT (a lamp keeping its whole light when a floor widens it, with no 60, and what it needs to be seen far off given by
//   place, as a size or a power that is the same at every distance): the power is here, 60 by place at every distance, but under
//   the ceiling the whole of it is shown only far off. It is what a sensor's ceiling does to a small bright thing; but a sensor's
//   ceiling is one for all the light in a pixel, and this is each lamp's own. The honest mend would be no ceiling of the lamp's
//   own, and is not made.
//   THE STREAK'S FLOOR IS ONE FOR THE WHOLE STREAK, taken at its farthest water, which the crew's note did not say: the nearer
//   water of a floored streak is so drawn wider than its own floor would ask, and as much dimmer (its light across is what its
//   lamp gives by place at every point of it). The honest mend would be each water's own floor with its light across still kept,
//   and is not made.
//   A MAST HAS NO FLOOR: it is drawn at its true thickness (0.06 to 0.32 m) at every distance from the lens. THE CREW'S NOTE SAID
//   MORE, that its cover would go to its share where it is thinner than a pixel, AND THAT IS NOT DONE: thinner than the pixel
//   named above (for a mast seen square on, beyond about 54 m for the thinnest and about 288 m for the thickest) a mast is drawn
//   only where one of a pixel's samples falls in it, and is to be expected to show as dashes that shift as the lens moves (the
//   crew's own expectation, masts-road/paper.txt and masts-road/refute.txt: it is not counted). The honest mend would be its
//   cover at its share; the crew found no clean way to it (a mast so blended is drawn after the rest, and the smoke, steam or
//   flame that stands before it is then not laid over it), and it is not made.
//   THE AIR BETWEEN: a lamp and its streak are dimmed by the scene's fog law (density 0.00035) taken at the lamp's straight
//   distance from the lens (a lamp's before its ceiling is taken, so a pixel still over the ceiling shows none of it). THE
//   SCENE'S OWN FOG, THE ROAD OF FIRE'S AND THE IMAGE'S GO BY THE DEPTH ALONG THE LENS'S AXIS, as three.js and the kit have it,
//   WHICH IS NOT THE LENGTH OF AIR: a thing at the middle of the upper or the lower edge of the view (31 degrees off the axis at
//   the typed field of 62 degrees) takes the fog of 0.86 of its straight distance, less toward the corners, and the fog on a
//   thing changes as the lens turns. KEPT, as the crew's note said it would be; the honest mend would be the straight distance in
//   every stage, the kit's among them, and is not made. The flames, the ember core and heart (and the ember shown through a
//   part-pitted pixel of the shell), the smoke, the steam, the sparks, the cascade, the glow in the air and the wake take no fog,
//   and the far mountains only their own haze: the sky's own colour low in their direction, going to the sky's glow colour where
//   a mountain stands toward the sun as seen from the eye in plan.
//   SHADINGS BY THE DIRECTION FROM A THING TO THE EYE, and no more than that: each sheet of the sleeve of flame is the outer face
//   of a body of flame 0.9 m deep (the near half of a round shell that lies under the sheet: nothing at the sheet, densest at its
//   inner face), and where there is flame its opacity is by how much of that body the ray to the eye crosses: 0.9 seen square on,
//   the whole toward the outline, where the ray runs longest through it, and nothing at the outline itself, where the ray only
//   touches the sheet; a tongue of flame stands upright and turns about the upright to face the eye (and streams: its tip is
//   drawn back to where its own anchor stood 0.35 s before, a law of T and not of the eye); a puff of smoke or steam, a spark and
//   a lamp face the lens (a puff is shaded as a soft lump bulging toward the lens, its slope built on the lens's own axes: a
//   shading by the lens's turn, no distance in it); a streak of the cascade lies along its own fall, seen side on; the sheet of
//   the fire's glow in the air is turned to the eye about the fire's own line; the fleet's, the figures' and the far mountains'
//   faces are shaded flat by each face's own slope taken across neighbouring pixels (the fleet's and the figures' turned to the
//   eye's side); the long ship's and the gulls' faces are lit with their slope turned to the eye's side; the shell shows char and
//   its whole ember where a face is seen from behind (the stack's inside); a gull's wing shows its grey top where the eye is on
//   its top side and its white under side where the eye is under it.
//   THE MIRROR: the road of fire and the stack's image on the sea, and the streak under each lamp, read the eye's place for the
//   mirrored ray: where the image lies and how much the water mirrors at that grazing angle. A STREAK IS NOT DRAWN, in one step,
//   where the eye stands almost under a high lamp: where 0.9 of the lamp's distance from the eye in plan (taken as no less than
//   1 m) is under 0.62 of the mirror point's distance from the lamp plus 0.5 m, which asks a lamp more than 1.8 times as high
//   over the sea as the eye and the eye within 1.8 m of it in plan; by place it is not met: on forty seeds no lamp stands nearer
//   the eye in plan than 14 m (streak/paper.txt). The honest mend would shorten the streak smoothly to nothing as its length
//   goes, in place of the step, and is not made. The road and the image lie on the kit sea's own waves (by its own uniforms and
//   its own clock) and on small ripples of the world's own. A streak's colour is 0.45 of its lamp's dealt colour (the lamp itself
//   is drawn at 2.6 of it) times its cover, broken into dashes; the cover's own bounds (its even part and its glint together 0.96
//   at the most, the dashes 0.2 to 1.5) allow 0.65 of that colour at the very most by the law, not counted. BY THE LAW a streak
//   cannot be brighter than its lamp's middle nearer than 15.5 times the distance at which the floor begins to widen the lamp's
//   glow (its dealt size over 0.0014; above); by place, on forty seeds, no lamp stands further from the lens than 9 times that
//   distance and no streak is over 0.14 of its lamp's middle (the same paper). Its band's half width is 0.2 m greater for each
//   metre its lamp hangs over the sea and it is as much dimmer; its middle wavers by up to one and a half times the band's half
//   width.
//   THE KIT'S OWN: the kit's dome is centred on the eye, its light rig follows it, and its sea's grid lies under the eye (a wave
//   its grid cannot hold left out, the sea's own mirror by the kit's law); the road of fire's mesh lies on that grid and follows
//   it. THE KIT'S OWN LAWS READ THE DISTANCE FROM THE LENS, each the kit's and not this world's to change. ITS GROUND shows its
//   pebbles and its tufts whole within 9 m of the lens and not at all from 95 m (a second layer of pebbles whole within 30 m,
//   gone from 280 m), and its bump is three times as strong within 5 m of the lens as from 60 m on; the lens comes no nearer to
//   dry ground than 32 m (ground/paper.txt), so that it meets both part way. ITS SEA dissolves into the sky seen in its direction
//   from 1,800 m to 3,492 m from the eye in plan, and its waves grow flatter from 1,080 m of its grid's middle and are flat from
//   2,880 m. THE LIGHT ITS SEA SCATTERS THROUGH ITS WAVE CRESTS (in the kit's own sea green: this world hands the kit no colour
//   for it and the kit's calm sea holds none) is whole at the lens and falls with the straight distance from it: about 0.77 of
//   itself at 100 m, about 0.56 at 250 m, about 0.39 at 500 m, and 0.3 far off (kitsea/paper.txt); how large it stands beside
//   the sea's own colour is not counted. The honest mend of each would be made in the kit (each layer of the ground and each
//   wave of the sea going to its own mean by the pixel's footprint, with no term of distance, the crests' light with none, and
//   a sea drawn as far as it is seen with only the air between; or, for its pebbles and tufts and for the crests' light, this
//   world handing the kit none) and none is made. THREE MORE OF THE KIT'S LAWS go by a pixel's footprint and not to the mean
//   under it; the crew's readers held them open and their weight in the frame is not measured: the sun's glitter on its sea
//   is dealt in cells three footprints wide, so that a sparkle is as large as its distance from the lens makes it; the fine
//   specks of its ground go to none, and not to their mean, as the footprint outgrows them, and the darkening they lay goes
//   with them; and the joints of its steep rock, and the veins and the ragged edge of its swash's foam at the shore, are left
//   out as the footprint outgrows them. Of steep ground there is little here: of 124,791 dry points of the ground's height,
//   taken 1.5 m apart to 400 m beyond the flown line's box, 44 have the upward part of the normal under 0.8, where the kit
//   begins to count ground as steep, and none under 0.55 (it counts it wholly steep at 0.5); the nearest of the 44 lies 32 m
//   from the flown line (kitsea/steep.out.txt). The honest mend of each, in the kit, would be its mean under the footprint,
//   and is not made.
//   NOTHING OF THE FIRE IS FADED, HIDDEN OR THINNED BY ITS NEARNESS TO THE LENS (of its distance the filters by a pixel's
//   footprint, the floors and the air between, named above, do read, and with them what is named there as kept or not mended: the
//   shell and the pallets, with the ember in them, stand under the scene's fog, the road of fire under its own). Clear air is
//   kept by place: until the release no spark comes nearer the flown line than 14.5 m, no puff of smoke than 23.5 m, no shed
//   pallet than 25.8 m (the sums in node, as of the law, not of the picture: every spark and every puff on eight seeds, every
//   other second of the burn, at sixteen chance clocks and at entry clocks of 0 to 60 s; the shed pallets' whole flights on forty
//   seeds).
//   THE GLOW IN THE AIR (a sheet of summed light about the fire's line, 0.10 of the fire's colour at the most) is nought at the
//   sea's level and whole from 8 m up, so that the sea cuts no edge in it.
//   THE FALL'S COUNTS go by THE MEASURE: a thing at the journey's second T is counted against the stretch of the flown line that
//   the eye flies within half a second of T. By it, in node, as of the law: the lengths' skin as drawn is never nearer than 28.5
//   m; a flame on the stack or on the lengths, at any second of the way, 24.5 m (sixty seeds); a spark of the burst 36.5 m (forty
//   seeds); a spark from the release on 24.7 m, a puff of smoke 22.4 m (eight seeds, sixteen chance clocks and entry clocks of 0
//   to 60 s); the steam's and the spray's edge 23.7 m (forty seeds; the hiss counted at twenty phases of each puff's turn); a
//   pallet of the wreck in flight 53.3 m and at rest 28.2 m from any point of the line still to fly (forty seeds). No hull lies
//   nearer the fallen line than 44.3 m (forty seeds); the ring wave over 0.01 strong is within 38 m of the fallen line's water.
//   SECTION 143 b asks, for a motion keyed to the metre flown, the lens's place for that metre: THE MEASURE is the stricter.
//   AGAINST THE WHOLE WAY FLOWN the fall is NOT clear and is not asked to be: the falling stack passes through air the eye flew
//   through some 33 s before (its axis within 5.1 m of the way's point at metre 133, 22.6 m up, at T 49.2; its skin as drawn
//   comes no nearer the way than 2.3 m, by the way's point at metre 135, 23.1 m up, at T 48.5).
//   THE GULLS go by the world's clock, and so THEIR WHOLE COURSE IS COUNTED AGAINST THE WHOLE FLOWN LINE (section 143 b): every
//   degree of every circle, the bob as flown and a wing's reach of 0.8 m taken off, against every half metre of the line, in
